JOB 29 – PROJECT SPRINT I: BUILD

1. Introduction
Job 29 introduces project development using Python. Instead of only learning programming syntax, students are required to identify a real-life problem and develop a useful software solution.
The project should solve a practical problem related to rural development or assistive technology.
2. Learning Objectives
Identify a real-life problem.
Understand the requirements of a project.
Select a suitable project idea.
Develop a working Python program.
Read input, process data and generate output.
Complete a small project within the given time.
3. Starting Points – Project Ideas
The following project ideas are given in the course material.
A. Rural Development Projects
1. Irrigation Logbook
Records pump running hours.
Records water delivery per plot.
Reports which plots are under-watered.
Identifies possible pump failures.
2. Storage Loss Tracker
Records weekly grain weights.
Calculates storage losses.
Ranks storage bags according to losses.
Helps farmers identify which storage method is effective.
B. Assistive Technology Projects
3. Medicine Reminder Sheet
Records medicine prescriptions.
Groups medicines according to time.
Provides warnings when two medicines may clash in timing.
Generates a printable medicine schedule.
4. Wheelchair Route Notes
Stores shared wheelchair routes.
Records ramps and door widths.
Provides information about accessible routes.
Helps users identify suitable paths.
C. Other Project Ideas
5. Your Own Shop-Floor Tool
A tool designed for a real workshop, such as:
Inspection bench.
Batch reporter.
Machine fleet monitor.
6. Bring Your Own Idea
Students can select any real-life problem and develop a suitable Python-based solution.
4. Scope Gauge – Project Planning
Before developing a project, its features must be selected according to the available time.
The project has approximately 10 working hours before the demonstration.
5. Three Main Stages of a Project
Every project should follow three important stages:
Input: Collect data from a file or user.
Process: Perform calculations and analyze the data.
Output: Display results or generate a report.
Example: Production Quality Checker
Input: Production measurements.
Process: Check measurements and identify defective products.
Output: Accepted and rejected product reports.
Conclusion
Job 29 focuses on identifying real-life problems and developing practical Python projects.
It teaches project planning, feature selection, problem-solving and building a working application within a limited time.
